{"step_index":88,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T10:36:29Z","content":"Priyanshu, here is the final, fully corrected version. This contains the exact diagrams and the exact algebra required to get 100% full marks on every question. Memorize this exactly as written.\n\n***\n\n**Q1. Derive the expression for Logarithmic Decrement ($\\delta$)**\n\n*   **DRAW THIS FIRST:** Draw an X-Y graph. Y-axis is Displacement ($x$), X-axis is Time ($t$). Draw a wavy sine wave that gets smaller over time. Label the first top peak \"$x_1$\" and mark the time below it as \"$t_1$\". Label the next top peak \"$x_2$\" and mark the time below it as \"$t_1 + t_d$\".\n*   **Definition:** It is the natural logarithm of the ratio of any two successive amplitudes on the same side of the mean position.\n\n**Derivation:**\n1. The equation for under-damped free vibration is: \n   $x = X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n t} \\sin(\\omega_d t + \\phi)$\n2. Let amplitude $x_1$ occur at time $t_1$:\n   $x_1 = X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n t_1}$\n3. The next successive amplitude $x_2$ occurs after one time period $t_d$:\n   $x_2 = X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n (t_1 + t_d)}$\n4. Take the ratio of $x_1$ to $x_2$:\n   $\\frac{x_1}{x_2} = \\frac{X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n t_1}}{X e^{-\\zeta \\omega_n (t_1 + t_d)}} = e^{\\zeta \\omega_n t_d}$\n5. Take the natural logarithm ($\\ln$) on both sides:\n   $\\delta = \\ln\\left(\\frac{x_1}{x_2}\\right) = \\zeta \\omega_n t_d$\n6. We know time period $t_d = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega_d}$ and damped frequency $\\omega_d = \\omega_n \\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}$.\n7. Substitute $t_d$:\n   $\\delta = \\zeta \\omega_n \\left( \\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega_n \\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}} \\right)$\n8. **Final Equation:** $\\delta = \\frac{2\\pi \\zeta}{\\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}}$\n\n***\n\n**Q2. Derive Natural Frequency ($\\omega_n$) by Energy Method**\n\n*   **DRAW THIS FIRST:** Draw a horizontal line representing a ceiling. Draw a coiled spring hanging down from it. Draw a square block attached to the bottom of the spring labeled \"$m$\". Draw an arrow pointing down from the block labeled \"$x$\" (displacement).\n*   **Principle:** In a conservative system (no damping), the total energy remains constant. Maximum Kinetic Energy = Maximum Potential Energy. ($K.E._{max} = P.E._{max}$)\n\n**Derivation:**\n1. Let mass $m$ have a simple harmonic displacement: $x = X \\sin(\\omega_n t)$\n2. Velocity $v = \\frac{dx}{dt} = X \\omega_n \\cos(\\omega_n t)$\n3. Maximum velocity occurs when $\\cos(\\omega_n t) = 1$: \n   $V_{max} = X \\omega_n$\n4. Maximum Kinetic Energy (of mass):\n   $K.E._{max} = \\frac{1}{2} m (V_{max})^2 = \\frac{1}{2} m X^2 \\omega_n^2$\n5. Maximum Potential Energy (of spring with stiffness $k$):\n   $P.E._{max} = \\frac{1}{2} k X^2$\n6. Equating both:\n   $\\frac{1}{2} m X^2 \\omega_n^2 = \\frac{1}{2} k X^2$\n7. Cancel $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $X^2$ from both sides:\n   $m \\omega_n^2 = k$\n8. **Final Equation:** $\\omega_n = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}} \\text{ (rad/s)}$\n\n***\n\n**Q3. Derive Turning Moment ($T$) from Piston Effort ($F_p$)**\n\n*   **DRAW THIS FIRST:** Draw a basic slider-crank mechanism. Label the crank radius as $r$ at angle $\\theta$. Label the connecting rod length as $L$ at angle $\\phi$. Label $n = L/r$. \n\n**Derivation:**\n1. Let $F_p$ = Net load on piston. Let $F_Q$ = Thrust force along the connecting rod.\n   $F_Q = \\frac{F_p}{\\cos \\phi}$ \n2. Let $F_T$ = Tangential force acting on the crank pin.\n   $F_T = F_Q \\sin(\\theta + \\phi) = \\frac{F_p}{\\cos \\phi} \\sin(\\theta + \\phi)$\n3. Turning Moment ($T$) at the crankshaft is Tangential Force $\\times$ Crank Radius:\n   $T = F_T \\times r = F_p \\times \\frac{\\sin(\\theta + \\phi)}{\\cos \\phi} \\times r$\n4. Expand the sine addition: \n   $T = F_p \\times r \\left[ \\frac{\\sin\\theta\\cos\\phi + \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi}{\\cos\\phi} \\right]$\n5. Divide by $\\cos\\phi$:\n   $T = F_p \\times r \\left[ \\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta \\frac{\\sin\\phi}{\\cos\\phi} \\right]$\n6. From mechanism geometry, we know $\\sin\\phi = \\frac{\\sin\\theta}{n}$ and $\\cos\\phi = \\frac{\\sqrt{n^2 - \\sin^2\\theta}}{n}$. Substitute these in:\n   $T = F_p \\times r \\left[ \\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta \\frac{\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{n}}{\\frac{\\sqrt{n^2 - \\sin^2\\theta}}{n}} \\right]$\n7. Cancel the $n$'s on the right side:\n   $T = F_p \\times r \\left[ \\sin\\theta + \\frac{\\cos\\theta \\sin\\theta}{\\sqrt{n^2 - \\sin^2\\theta}} \\right]$\n8. Use the math identity $2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta = \\sin 2\\theta$ (multiply top and bottom by 2):\n   **Final Equation:** $T = F_p \\cdot r \\left[ \\sin\\theta + \\frac{\\sin 2\\theta}{2\\sqrt{n^2 - \\sin^2\\theta}} \\right]$\n\n***\n\n**Q4. Define Damping Types (Under, Over, Critical)**\n\n*   **DRAW THIS FIRST:** Draw an X-Y graph (Y=Displacement, X=Time). Start 3 curves from high up on the Y-axis. 1) Draw an oscillating wave decaying to zero and label it $\\zeta < 1$. 2) Draw a steep, smooth drop hitting zero without crossing it, label it $\\zeta = 1$. 3) Draw a slow, lazy drop to zero, label it $\\zeta > 1$.\n\n1. **Under-damped ($\\zeta < 1$):** The roots are complex conjugates. The system vibrates with an exponentially decaying amplitude until it stops. \n2. **Critically damped ($\\zeta = 1$):** The roots are real and equal. The system returns to its equilibrium position in the shortest possible time without any oscillation.\n3. **Over-damped ($\\zeta > 1$):** The roots are real and distinct. The system is sluggish and slowly returns to equilibrium without oscillation.\n\n***\n\n**Q5. Dynamically Equivalent System**\n\nTo replace a rigid continuous body (like a connecting rod of mass $m$) with two concentrated point masses ($m_1$ and $m_2$) placed at distances $l_1$ and $l_2$ from the Center of Gravity, three exact conditions must be met:\n\n1. **Mass Condition:** $m_1 + m_2 = m$\n2. **Center of Gravity Condition:** $m_1 l_1 = m_2 l_2$\n3. **Inertia Condition:** $m_1 l_1^2 + m_2 l_2^2 = I$\n\n***\n\n**Q6. Magnification Factor & Transmissibility**\n\n1. **Magnification Factor (M.F.):** The ratio of the maximum dynamic amplitude ($X_{max}$) to the static deflection under a static force ($X_{static} = F_0 / k$). \n   $M.F. = \\frac{X_{max}}{X_{static}}$\n2. **Transmissibility ($\\epsilon$):** The ratio of the force actually transmitted to the foundation ($F_T$) to the disturbing force applied to the machine ($F_0$).\n   $\\epsilon = \\frac{F_T}{F_0}$\n   *(Condition for isolation: frequency ratio $\\omega/\\omega_n$ must be greater than $\\sqrt{2}$).*\n\n***\n\n**Q7. Coefficient of Fluctuation of Speed & Energy**\n\n1. **Coefficient of Fluctuation of Speed ($C_s$):** The ratio of the maximum fluctuation of speed to the mean speed.\n   $C_s = \\frac{N_{max} - N_{min}}{N_{mean}}$\n2. **Coefficient of Fluctuation of Energy ($C_E$):** The ratio of the maximum fluctuation of energy to the total work done per cycle.\n   $C_E = \\frac{\\Delta E}{\\text{Work done per cycle}}$","thinking":"**Revisiting the Prompt**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the core request: reintegrating the diagrams and algebraic components into the \"cheat sheet.\" The focus is on a comprehensive, self-contained output, eliminating the need for external references.\n\n\n"}
